## From ID to a list of numbers

<At levels="simple">

A token's number is just a label: 3797 says nothing about cats. So GPT-2 swaps each token for a
long list of numbers, called an <GlossaryPopover term="embedding">embedding</GlossaryPopover>.
Step through to see the running example become lists of numbers. (Only the first 48 numbers are
drawn; striped cells are negative.)

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A token ID is just a label: 3797 for *" cat"* says nothing about cats. So the first thing GPT-2 does
with each token is look up a list of 768 numbers for it, called an <GlossaryPopover term="embedding">embedding</GlossaryPopover> or a vector. Step through to see the
running example become vectors. (Only the first 48 numbers are drawn; striped cells are negative.)

</At>

<TokenToVector />

Nobody chose these numbers. They start out random and are adjusted during training, which Part III
explains. After training, tokens that are used in similar ways end up with similar
lists.<Cite id="radford2019" />

<At levels="deep">

### A lookup table

GPT-2 keeps every token's vector in one table, $W_E$, with a row for each of its 50,257 tokens and
768 columns. Turning token $t$ into its vector $e_t$ just reads row $t$:

$$
e_t = W_E[t] = \mathbf{1}_t^{\top} W_E
$$

where $\mathbf{1}_t$ is a list of 50,257 zeros with a single 1 in place $t$. Multiplying by it
picks out one row, which is why training can adjust the table like any other weights. The table
holds 50,257 × 768 = 38,597,376 numbers, about 31% of GPT-2 small's 124 million weights, and GPT-2
uses it twice: at the end of the model, the same table turns the final vector back into a score for
every token.

</At>

## Similar meanings, nearby points

<At levels="simple">

A list of numbers can be read as a place: two numbers mark a point on a flat map, three a point in
a room, and longer lists mark points in a space with more directions. Nearby points mean similar
things.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A list of numbers can be read as a position. Two numbers place a point on a flat map, and three
place it in a room; 384 or 768 numbers place it in a space with that many directions. Nearby points have similar lists, and similar
lists turn out to mean similar things.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

The map below uses a small model built for comparing meanings.<Cite id="minilm-model-card" />
Pick a word to see its nearest neighbors, and drag the map to turn it.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The map below uses all-MiniLM-L6-v2, a small model built for comparing meanings, which turns any
text into 384 numbers.<Cite id="minilm-model-card" /> Pick a word to see its nearest neighbors, and
drag the map (or use the sliders) to turn it and see it from another side.

</At>

<EmbeddingMap />

<At levels="simple">

The map squashes many numbers down to three directions, so a few neighbors may look far apart on
it.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The map is a squashed view. Squashing 384 numbers down to three directions loses most of the
detail, so a few neighbors may look far apart on the map, and turning it shows words that looked
close from one side sitting far apart from another. The lists of neighbors and similarity scores use all 384
numbers.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Measuring "nearby"

The neighbors are ranked by **cosine similarity**, which compares the directions of two vectors $a$
and $b$ and ignores their lengths:

$$
\cos(a, b) = \frac{a \cdot b}{\lVert a \rVert \, \lVert b \rVert}, \qquad a \cdot b = \sum_{i} a_i b_i, \qquad \lVert a \rVert = \sqrt{a \cdot a}
$$

where $a_i$ is the $i$-th number in $a$. It is 1 when the vectors point the same way, 0 when they
are at right angles, and −1 when they point opposite ways; *cat* and *kitten* score 0.79. This
model's vectors all have length 1, so here cosine similarity is just the sum $a \cdot b$.

The map comes from principal component analysis: it keeps the three directions along which
these words' vectors spread out the most, and drops the other 381. Those three directions hold
only about 18% of the spread, which is why neighbors on the map can mislead.

</At>

## Directions mean something too

<At levels="simple">

Tick *Show king − man + woman*. Start at *king*, take away *man*, add *woman*, and the nearest
word is *queen*. So directions in the space can carry meaning, like the step from *man* to
*woman*. It doesn't work for every pair of words.<Cite id="mikolov2013" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Tick *Show king − man + woman*. Taking the vector for *king*, subtracting *man*, and adding *woman*
gives a list of numbers whose nearest word on this map, comparing all 384 numbers, is *queen*. That
works because the step from *man* to *woman* partly lines up with the step from *king* to *queen*:
the two are about 75° apart in all 384 numbers, where parallel would be 0°. That's still closer
than most steps between these words. So don't expect parallel arrows on the map; their angle
changes as you turn it.
This famous example was first shown in
2013 with word vectors from a model called word2vec.<Cite id="mikolov2013" /> It doesn't work for
every analogy, and on this map it only has a few dozen words to choose from, but it shows that
directions in the space can carry meaning.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

In vectors, the search finds the word whose vector is most similar to

$$
v_{\text{king}} - v_{\text{man}} + v_{\text{woman}}
$$

by cosine similarity, where $v_x$ is the vector for word $x$.<Cite id="mikolov2013" /> The search
skips the three words that went in. Counted with them, the nearest word is *king* itself, at 0.63
against 0.58 for *queen*: the sum lands close to where it started, and *queen* is only the best of
the rest.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Each token is turned into a vector: a list of numbers (768 for GPT-2 small).
- The numbers are learned, and tokens used in similar ways end up with similar vectors.
- Similar vectors are nearby points, and some directions carry meaning, like *man* to *woman*.

<At levels="deep">

- An embedding is a row of a learned table; nearness is cosine similarity, the angle between
  vectors.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** a model turns these vectors into a chance for every next token. What is that
calculation made of, and where do its numbers come from?
